{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1780,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How much energy is there in empty space ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4780,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We believe there is 10 to the minus 8th ergs in every cubic centimeter of empty space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9220,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How much is an \"erg\" ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10560,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's a very small amount, you know if a... If a mosquito flaps its wings, or a fly I guess"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15020,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flaps its wings, it's about 1 ergs worth of energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17780,
    "dDurationMs": 1420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So an erg is not very much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19340,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Uhh, we're talking about a hundred millionth of an erg in every cubic centimeter of space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24420,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, it might seem strange that completely empty space contains energy, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29120,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there are 2 good reasons why scientists think this is the case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32400,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One is because the expansion of the universe is speeding up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 35840,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that's thought to be caused by dark energy,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38140,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which fills all space and makes gravity repulsive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41140,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We always think that gravity always pulls, but according to Einstein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44660,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way gravity works really depends on the material itself and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48420,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy tied to space, gravity pushes, it doesn't pull."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52680,
    "dDurationMs": 3740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that number is 10 to the minus 8th ergs, per cubic centimeter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56420,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then you fit the data, then that's the dark energy that makes universe accelerate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59700,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's constant, does everything you want."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61700,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second reason why empty space contains energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64680,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is because it isn't really \"empty\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66760,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is the idea that, thanks to quantum mechanics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69020,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we do expect particles to come in and out of existence, and so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72820,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space is full of energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74380,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah, there was nothing there when we first looked and nothing there when we later looked"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78040,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Could we say for sure, if we had looked in the middle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81120,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that there weren't particles there ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82480,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No, there might have been"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84660,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Evidence for these so-called \"virtual particles\" came from studying the light emitted by energized hydrogen atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91180,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Niels Bohr first used the particular colors of light to divise his quantum model of the atom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96980,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where the electron orbits the nucleus in certain allowed orbits, each with its own specific energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102060,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the electron jumps from a higher-energy orbit to a lower one, it gives off light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the energy enhanced color of that light is determined by the energy difference between the two levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111420,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As quantum theory developed, Bohr's orbits were superseded by probablistic electron clouds,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116780,
    "dDurationMs": 2980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the energy levels remained mostly unchanged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120040,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then, in 1947, Willis Lamb and his grad student Robert Retherford"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124460,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "attempted to test the best version of quantum theory at the time, which was the Dirac equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 130040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they did this by measuring the energy levels in hydrogen more precisely than ever before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135780,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, what they found did not match up with the theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the energy levels was actually not one, but rather two, very closely spaced energy levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146500,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is known as the Lamb shift."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149020,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They used microwaves to excite the electron between these two levels."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152860,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, why were there two energy levels where there was supposed to be just one ?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157260,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is Virtual Particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159560,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example around the proton virtual electron-positron pairs pop in and out of existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165540,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is permitted by the laws of Quantum Mechanics so long as they don't stick around for too long."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170060,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During their brief lives the negative electron is attracted to the positive proton while the positron is repelled away from it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176480,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This means the charge of the proton is shielded by the  virtual electrons surrounding it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 181080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This affects the energy of the 2s orbital - in which the electron is closer to the nucleus -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186120,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "differently from the 2p orbital, and that's what causes the energy levels to split."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191120,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is just one contribution to the energy difference."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194260,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The full calculation involves drawing a series of Feynman diagrams"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197900,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that show all the possible interactions between real and virtual particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201980,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and adding up the contribution from each one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204480,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there's an infinite chain of these different diagrams."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208380,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you might worry that you can predict nothing at all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210720,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because you'd need to add up an infinite number of numbers to get the right answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213840,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the point is that every time you have these two... vertices"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218140,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these two places where the photon hits the electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that's associated with a number alpha, the fine-structure constant, which is 1/137, which is a small number."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228100,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So,  this leading term, this classical contribution is already small"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233560,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these more-complicated ones, you notice there are more vertices up here"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238400,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and as the diagrams get more and more complicated there are more and more of these terms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242300,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so you get alpha squared; alpha cubed; alpha to the fourth, and the numbers become smaller and smaller and smaller"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248720,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the more complicated diagrams"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the more intricate effects of the virtual particles become less and less important."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Using the Dirac equation, but adding in the effects of virtual electrons, positrons, and photons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262160,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can actually calculate the values of atomic parameters with better accuracy than 1 part in a billion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269220,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is incredible. In fact, physicists Lawrence Krauss calls it: \"The best and most accurate prediction in all of science.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276940,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So with the spectacular usefulness of virtual particles"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280780,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You think it would be pretty hard for anyone to doubt their existence,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284340,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then again, they have never been directly observed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288000,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, theoretically, that's impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290980,
    "dDurationMs": 10760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Explaining virtual particles is always tricky, because it's very hard to think of them in any sort of real way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301740,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They can go in the opposite direction of their momenta, and... and..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305340,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And do these things which are not consistent with... with a real particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309840,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So virtual particles have a strange sort of existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313320,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Where they're indispensable for calculations, obviously,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316260,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but you can never directly observe them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319340,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But maybe this is not actually a real problem, it's just the result of thinking about reality in the wrong way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326400,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maybe as the fundamental building blocks of our universe, we shouldn't be talking about particles at all;"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332060,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but rather, \"fields.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333580,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The truth is that Quantum Field Theory is, according to our best current modern understanding, the right way to talk about nature at a fundamental level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341860,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Uh... When we talk about particle physics, when we talk about electrons and gluons and quarks..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346440,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To the modern physicist, these are vibrations in fields. In exactly the same way the photon is a vibration in the elctromagnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354420,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just like there is an electromagnetic field, there is also:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356520,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an electron field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358260,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's a neutrino field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359820,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there's and up-quark field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360840,
    "dDurationMs": 1040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a top-quark field,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361880,
    "dDurationMs": 940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "et caetera."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362820,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And these fields fill space, and usually they're just sitting there, fairly quietly, but when they vibrate, and you look at it, you see that in terms of individual particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370740,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this picture, virtual particles are quantum fluctuations."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374580,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Little disturbances in the field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376900,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They play a role in interactions,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378740,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they aren't measurable as 'real particles,' themselves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381740,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And since they exist everywhere,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383760,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should imbue the vacuum with energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386140,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you were to try to estimate the amount of energy due to the virtual particles in empty space,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391000,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what kind of number do you get for the energy of empty space?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393660,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yeah, this is a huge problem,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395320,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because the 'back-of-the-envelope' estimate says there should be about 10^112 erg for every cubic centimeter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401680,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to the 10^-8 that we actually get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, you predict 10^120 times the energy density of empty space that you actually observe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411640,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So obviously your 'back-of-the-envelope' calculation is wildly, wildly incorrect and no one is really quite sure why."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The way we said the universe responding to the cosmological constant the way we expect the vacuum to behave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423680,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are the most discrepants things we have in physics,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426500,
    "dDurationMs": 1340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if they are the same things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428040,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How is it possible that both the best and the worst prediction in science are both based on the same underlying physics of virtual particles?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436280,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean, one experiment seems to show that the theory is perfectly correct,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440520,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while another shows that we could not be more wrong."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444460,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You know, there are a lot of areas of human endeavor where we fear being so wildly incorrect,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451140,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but in science, this discrepancy is actually really exciting."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456000,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it shows us a piece of the puzzle that we haven't figured out just yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459940,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That we have to solve this problem if we are to fully understand the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464860,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in that way, this is the best clue we have at the work that still needs to be done."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470740,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So...yeah.We don't know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473200,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that's always good! It's always good to say you don't know, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476800,
    "dDurationMs": 800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think so!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey! This episode of Veritasium was supported by viewers like you on Patreon and by The Great Courses Plus,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485200,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is an awesome online-learning platform with thousands of video-courses in all different areas,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490720,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "including one I'm particularly excited about, which is on the Higgs boson, taught by my friend Shawn Carrol, who you saw featured in this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497680,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, in it, he actually dedicates one whole lecture to field theory, so if you want to do deeper into the things I was talking about here, then you want to check out his course."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506360,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you can actually watch his whole course and all the others for free, for one-month free trial, by going to:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511980,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thegreatcoursesplus.com/veritasium, or just click the link in the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516600,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, I really want to think The Great Courses Plus for supporting me, and I want to thank you for watching."
    } ]
  } ]
}
